00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:20.170
- Well, definitely looking forward to it

00:01:20.170 --> 00:01:21.710
and just knowing your work

00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:24.250
and the conversations that
you've had over the years,

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:26.550
your book, your upcoming book as well,

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:29.310
I think this is just gonna
be a great conversation

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:30.430
and topic, a lot of our listeners

00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:33.390
will really be able to
engage with as well.

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:35.010
So as you know, as our listeners know,

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:36.530
I always like to start with a check-in.

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:37.630
So just wanted to start there

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:39.750
and just see how you're doing these days

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:41.600
in the midst of everything

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:43.320
that we're all dealing with as a society.

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:45.650
And just the things that
even walking through

00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:47.520
in your personal life as well.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:52.520
- Yeah, it's a lot of ups
and downs, I can't lie.

00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:55.360
And I would honestly question anybody

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:58.150
who's not feeling some
amount of ups and downs

00:01:58.150 --> 00:02:00.890
and you know my hope in my own growth

00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:03.210
is to find equanimity at some point

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:07.483
but I don't got that at this exact moment.

00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:13.620
So that said, I'm finding
myself better and better

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:19.470
able not to be trapped
in my own thought loops

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:21.663
and stories and anxieties.

00:02:23.277 --> 00:02:26.060
And by becoming better at accepting them,

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:28.700
accepting that they are
just what is happening

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:29.880
and that that's not bad.

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:30.950
It doesn't make me bad.

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:32.800
It doesn't make all my
healing all for not,

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:36.540
like it's not some
catastrophe it just kinda is,

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:39.150
the more and more I get
comfortable with that

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:41.680
the easier and easier it
is to loosen the grip.

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:42.630
And then all of a sudden,

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:45.910
I'm not in some like anxiety spiral.

00:02:45.910 --> 00:02:48.070
So I'd say ups and downs

00:02:48.070 --> 00:02:52.200
but overall actually a lot more resilient

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:57.070
to the different moods
my brain and emotions

00:02:57.070 --> 00:03:02.070
can fling me into and so yeah, mixed bag.

00:03:03.687 --> 00:03:05.120
- And I appreciate that honesty.

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:05.980
And I think it's interesting

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:08.040
that you put those two together too.

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:09.050
'Cause you talk about

00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:11.830
the honesty of accepting
that they're there

00:03:11.830 --> 00:03:14.030
but then also still
trying to build resilience

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:14.930
at the same time.

00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:16.090
So it's not like you're just accepting

00:03:16.090 --> 00:03:18.340
and saying, oh, well
that's just the way it is

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:20.920
and there's nothing that
can be done about it.

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:22.525
- What I find is,

00:03:22.525 --> 00:03:27.525
if I don't accept it, if I
resist it then I really suffer.

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:30.970
Then it has a texture of something like

00:03:30.970 --> 00:03:33.480
really that's pulling me under.

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:35.875
But when I just say, oh, it is what it is

00:03:35.875 --> 00:03:38.720
and that's not my entire life,

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:41.170
that's just this one little slice

00:03:42.459 --> 00:03:47.050
that has become a richer and
richer way to just calm down.

00:03:47.050 --> 00:03:50.400
Because the circumstances
outside are crazy

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:55.110
like even though the pandemic
seems to be in a better place

00:03:55.110 --> 00:03:58.140
than it was, like what
does that even mean?

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:01.030
A better place in a global pandemic

00:04:01.030 --> 00:04:02.960
of death and destruction?

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:05.710
Like this is kind of
a worst case scenario.

00:04:05.710 --> 00:04:10.710
So I'm just trying to be a
little, cut myself a little slack

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:13.530
for the fact that I'm
not a hundred percent

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:15.330
every single day.

00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:17.270
- Yeah, that seems so
healthy in a lot of ways,

00:04:17.270 --> 00:04:18.750
I mean, you put it in a
great perspective too.

00:04:18.750 --> 00:04:21.520
I mean, the pandemic is
still going on, it's better

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:23.910
but it's not as if we're
completely (indistinct),

00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:26.890
so there's still--
- Yeah, like it's good

00:04:26.890 --> 00:04:28.530
for a global pandemic.

00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:30.760
Like that's not a real phrase.

00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:32.640
Like one we would you have
ever said that before,

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:35.870
like, oh, I'm doing good for
being in a global pandemic.

00:04:35.870 --> 00:04:36.980
Like that's crazy.

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:38.310
- Yeah, but yet at the same time

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:39.570
where I know I'm talking like that,

00:04:39.570 --> 00:04:41.230
a lot of us were just
all talking that way.

00:04:41.230 --> 00:04:44.280
So it's just kind of the
reality for the moment too.

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:46.010
And I think you know within the pandemic

00:04:46.010 --> 00:04:48.190
even this idea that you've
talked about the acceptance,

00:04:48.190 --> 00:04:49.680
it seems like there's
a lot more acceptance

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:53.180
of how anxiety provoking this
has been across the board.

00:04:53.180 --> 00:04:54.480
I mean, other situations I've said

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:57.819
in some ways we've almost
gone through a global trauma

00:04:57.819 --> 00:04:59.730
just in terms of not knowing

00:04:59.730 --> 00:05:01.500
exactly how things would play out

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:03.430
and all the anxiety that comes with that.

00:05:03.430 --> 00:05:04.710
So I think for a lot of people

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:06.700
who may not have been able to relate

00:05:06.700 --> 00:05:08.240
as well to anxiety beforehand,

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:10.760
this became somewhat of an equalizer,

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:11.593
I mean, that's total understanding

00:05:11.593 --> 00:05:13.290
because it also heightened
some of the disparities

00:05:13.290 --> 00:05:16.020
were already there by the sense that

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:17.090
globally we're kind of dealing

00:05:17.090 --> 00:05:18.560
with this uncertainty and anxiety,

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:22.050
I think that's been huge
for us as a society.

00:05:22.050 --> 00:05:24.030
But even with the idea of acceptance,

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:26.430
why do you think even
though maybe it's easier

00:05:26.430 --> 00:05:27.490
for people to accept now,

00:05:27.490 --> 00:05:30.590
why do you think it's just
so difficult for us to accept

00:05:30.590 --> 00:05:32.190
when things are actually challenging?

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:33.620
I mean, it seems like it's taboo,

00:05:33.620 --> 00:05:36.600
we can't talk about it,
we pretend that it's fine.

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:39.670
Like we're, what's going on there?

00:05:39.670 --> 00:05:41.730
- It's a funny thing and it's
something I'm writing about

00:05:41.730 --> 00:05:44.730
you know like when you're
in line at a grocery store

00:05:44.730 --> 00:05:46.040
and there's like a woman behind you

00:05:46.040 --> 00:05:48.490
and she's in tears, completely upset

00:05:48.490 --> 00:05:49.410
and you're like, "Are you okay?"

00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:50.968
And she's like, "I'm fine."

00:05:50.968 --> 00:05:54.380
And you're like, "I'm pretty
sure you're not fine."

00:05:54.380 --> 00:05:56.010
And like, it's pretty weird

00:05:56.010 --> 00:05:58.820
that our gut reaction
just deny, deny, deny,

00:05:58.820 --> 00:06:00.990
I'm fine, distance from this.

00:06:00.990 --> 00:06:03.030
And I think there are a
couple of things going on,

00:06:03.030 --> 00:06:05.933
I think on the societal level,

00:06:07.230 --> 00:06:10.720
and particularly in America,
we have this, the individual,

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:13.460
the lone individual, who's
like gonna do it on their own.

00:06:13.460 --> 00:06:16.410
And when we sell ourselves that myth

00:06:16.410 --> 00:06:20.120
and we're supposed to be so
strong and never ask for help.

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:25.120
And then my book is half
memoir, half self-help.

00:06:25.670 --> 00:06:28.420
And I remember when I was
talking to the marketing people

00:06:28.420 --> 00:06:29.890
and they told me,

00:06:29.890 --> 00:06:31.970
well, it's gonna be in
the self-help category.

00:06:31.970 --> 00:06:35.380
I was like, euh, I don't
wanna be a self-help writer.

00:06:35.380 --> 00:06:38.020
Like that's so cheesy and
gross and terrible and lame.

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:40.460
And I really had to check myself,

00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:44.510
why is it bad to help yourself?

00:06:44.510 --> 00:06:46.500
Like the people who have the power

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:50.570
to pick up a self-help
book should be lauded.

00:06:50.570 --> 00:06:53.010
Like those are the powerful strong people

00:06:53.010 --> 00:06:55.387
who are taking a step back and saying,

00:06:55.387 --> 00:06:59.000
"I have an issue and I'm
strong enough to deal with it."

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:03.180
So I think we're fed a
bunch of lies about our,

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:06.910
how we need to do things
alone, I think that's one part.

00:07:06.910 --> 00:07:09.510
And the other part I think is,

00:07:09.510 --> 00:07:11.770
we use terms like I'm fine,

00:07:11.770 --> 00:07:13.760
I'm okay, when we're genuinely not

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:16.610
so that we can distance
ourselves from what's real.

00:07:16.610 --> 00:07:20.800
Like it's I think the defense
mechanism a lot of the time

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:24.710
to reject the reality,
reject the pain, I'm fine.

00:07:24.710 --> 00:07:27.220
Moving on, I don't wanna talk about it.

00:07:27.220 --> 00:07:30.060
And it also gives everyone
in our lives cover

00:07:30.060 --> 00:07:32.330
because then they don't
have to be intimate

00:07:32.330 --> 00:07:34.200
with our problems either.

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:37.540
And then they don't have
to face their own issues

00:07:37.540 --> 00:07:40.470
that are issues might kick up.

00:07:40.470 --> 00:07:44.530
And it just puts us all
in a terrible position

00:07:44.530 --> 00:07:49.240
because then everybody's
lying, like nothing is true.

00:07:49.240 --> 00:07:52.290
And people feel like they can't
reach out and ask for help

00:07:52.290 --> 00:07:54.980
because they don't think
anybody else is experiencing

00:07:54.980 --> 00:07:56.590
what they're experiencing

00:07:56.590 --> 00:08:00.225
because what we're fed is that
we should all just be fine.

00:08:00.225 --> 00:08:04.810
So that's sort of my take on
why we default to I'm fine.

00:08:04.810 --> 00:08:06.670
Which also by the way, is so lame.

00:08:06.670 --> 00:08:10.870
Like I don't wanna live my
life as fine, it's so sad.

00:08:10.870 --> 00:08:13.883
Like wanna feel something.

00:08:15.679 --> 00:08:16.833
- And that's so important.

00:08:16.833 --> 00:08:20.870
I think like you said, it
leaves a stigma in place too.

00:08:20.870 --> 00:08:22.090
Some of our earlier episodes

00:08:22.090 --> 00:08:23.620
we were talking with a NFL player

00:08:23.620 --> 00:08:25.610
and he was saying some of
the same types of things

00:08:25.610 --> 00:08:27.310
in terms of nobody wanna talk about it,

00:08:27.310 --> 00:08:29.100
everybody going through it themselves.

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:30.930
But once you get everyone in the room

00:08:30.930 --> 00:08:32.470
actually talking about it

00:08:32.470 --> 00:08:34.050
and they're all like realizing

00:08:34.050 --> 00:08:36.190
that they're all dealing
with the same thing,

00:08:36.190 --> 00:08:37.660
they thought they were the only one.

00:08:37.660 --> 00:08:40.600
Then (indistinct) this
collective group theory

00:08:40.600 --> 00:08:42.020
therapy type of moment

00:08:42.020 --> 00:08:44.490
where people can actually
walk through things together

00:08:44.490 --> 00:08:45.670
and actually learn from each other.

00:08:45.670 --> 00:08:47.467
So I taught so and so forth.
- Yeah, totally.

00:08:47.467 --> 00:08:50.110
And I think we're kind
of in a societal group

00:08:50.110 --> 00:08:55.110
therapy moment, you know that
there's great disparities

00:08:55.300 --> 00:08:57.050
about what kinds of suffering

00:08:57.050 --> 00:08:59.590
and what disadvantages people have.

00:08:59.590 --> 00:09:02.540
And there's a lot packed within that

00:09:02.540 --> 00:09:06.030
but if you are a human on
planet earth currently,

00:09:06.030 --> 00:09:08.980
our current circumstances are not ideal.

00:09:08.980 --> 00:09:11.620
So to add to some extent

00:09:11.620 --> 00:09:14.820
everybody is kind of being
confronted with this.

00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:19.120
And I think even having publicly athletes,

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:21.520
like the football player you mentioned

00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:26.520
or you know this week the tennis player

00:09:26.660 --> 00:09:29.250
in a French Open dropping out
for her own mental health,

00:09:29.250 --> 00:09:31.980
like having examples
of people saying like,

00:09:31.980 --> 00:09:34.860
hey wait, my mental health is my priority

00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:37.200
without that there's nothing else.

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:40.020
I mean, this wouldn't have
happened two years ago,

00:09:40.020 --> 00:09:43.280
you know, people were not this open.

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:47.110
So I think it might be an
unintended good consequence

00:09:47.110 --> 00:09:49.640
of the pandemic is just,

00:09:49.640 --> 00:09:52.870
it ripped off so much of the artifice

00:09:52.870 --> 00:09:55.960
and so much of the like,
everything is fine.

00:09:55.960 --> 00:09:59.290
We jus patchworked over this whole thing

00:09:59.290 --> 00:10:00.410
and doesn't it look nice?

00:10:00.410 --> 00:10:03.660
And it's like, no, it doesn't,
the insights are rotten.

00:10:03.660 --> 00:10:06.490
- Exactly, yeah, and I
noticed even the timing

00:10:06.490 --> 00:10:07.710
of your book released too.

00:10:07.710 --> 00:10:09.280
I'm sure you weren't predicting that

00:10:09.280 --> 00:10:11.780
and potentially planning to release a book

00:10:11.780 --> 00:10:12.613
during the pandemic

00:10:12.613 --> 00:10:15.810
but how do you think that
affected like how it was received?

00:10:15.810 --> 00:10:19.439
Do you think there was a tie in there?

00:10:19.439 --> 00:10:24.439
- I 100%, I mean, obviously
you work so hard on a book,

00:10:25.480 --> 00:10:30.140
you know in some ways that
book took me 30 years to write,

00:10:30.140 --> 00:10:33.030
it took three dedicated years

00:10:33.030 --> 00:10:35.740
but 30 of thinking about the book.

00:10:35.740 --> 00:10:38.300
And I was supposed to go
on like a big book tour

00:10:38.300 --> 00:10:40.480
and bought a dress to wear.

00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:43.820
And like that dress is
still sitting in my closet,

00:10:43.820 --> 00:10:44.960
a year and a half later,

00:10:44.960 --> 00:10:47.630
like who knows when I'm
gonna wear that dress.

00:10:47.630 --> 00:10:49.720
So that was different.

00:10:49.720 --> 00:10:50.840
That was an adjustment

00:10:50.840 --> 00:10:54.570
and that had its own
amount of grief for me

00:10:54.570 --> 00:10:57.510
because it wasn't how I
pictured things going.

00:10:57.510 --> 00:11:00.020
And it wasn't what I expected.

00:11:00.020 --> 00:11:03.000
And the book coming out in the pandemic

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:06.350
was the perfect time
for this exact message

00:11:06.350 --> 00:11:11.150
because people desperately needed some,

00:11:11.150 --> 00:11:13.730
with so much structure
taken out of their lives.

00:11:13.730 --> 00:11:15.800
It was actually the
perfect time to build in

00:11:15.800 --> 00:11:18.680
some good habits and
everything in my book,

00:11:18.680 --> 00:11:20.530
you know my book is about self-care

00:11:20.530 --> 00:11:23.710
and how to bring small
rituals into your life

00:11:23.710 --> 00:11:28.650
that hope you enjoy your
life and be present for it.

00:11:28.650 --> 00:11:33.650
People had time to try these
things, if for example,

00:11:34.090 --> 00:11:35.880
if you weren't commuting
to the office anymore

00:11:35.880 --> 00:11:38.113
that meant you had 20 minutes to journal.

00:11:40.280 --> 00:11:43.630
And because everything in my
book is free or super low cost

00:11:43.630 --> 00:11:47.400
there were also just no
barriers to entry there.

00:11:47.400 --> 00:11:50.290
And it helped that the
book was sold in target.

00:11:50.290 --> 00:11:55.010
So while you were like grabbing
all your toilet and beans,

00:11:55.010 --> 00:11:59.440
you could pick up this pretty
book with flowers on it.

00:11:59.440 --> 00:12:04.150
Yeah, and so in this really messed up way,

00:12:04.150 --> 00:12:06.600
it was the perfect time
for this book to come out.

00:12:06.600 --> 00:12:09.920
And I think I probably reached
out much broader audience

00:12:09.920 --> 00:12:12.080
than I otherwise would have.

00:12:12.080 --> 00:12:15.380
And I don't, I'm not happy that,

00:12:15.380 --> 00:12:17.000
you know who was your (indistinct).

00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:19.690
Like, I don't, I'm not
like the kind of person

00:12:19.690 --> 00:12:22.440
who needs to look for silver
linings and everything.

00:12:22.440 --> 00:12:24.630
I just think both things are true.

00:12:24.630 --> 00:12:28.910
Like it was sad that it happened in a way

00:12:28.910 --> 00:12:32.820
that I didn't expect and
it, amazing and wonderful.

00:12:32.820 --> 00:12:35.710
And I've been able to
connect with so many people

00:12:35.710 --> 00:12:38.820
and hopefully be helpful in a time

00:12:38.820 --> 00:12:40.680
where I would really want to be helpful.

00:12:40.680 --> 00:12:43.490
Like this is the time
to serve your community.

00:12:43.490 --> 00:12:46.040
If not now then when you know?

00:12:46.040 --> 00:12:47.067
- Yeah, it's so important.

00:12:47.067 --> 00:12:48.920
So, the investment I
think is so worthwhile

00:12:48.920 --> 00:12:50.650
for so many people.

00:12:50.650 --> 00:12:53.000
And even you know as you're
talking through this,

00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:54.400
I'm just curious to you about,

00:12:54.400 --> 00:12:56.160
how you even got yourself to that place

00:12:56.160 --> 00:12:57.190
of putting a book together,

00:12:57.190 --> 00:12:59.690
especially with your
initial kind of reaction

00:12:59.690 --> 00:13:02.210
to the whole self-care mode.

00:13:02.210 --> 00:13:03.600
And that's again to a place of,

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:04.650
I mean, the way you're talking now

00:13:04.650 --> 00:13:07.040
really wanting to invest
in people and the community

00:13:07.040 --> 00:13:08.060
and kind of empower people,

00:13:08.060 --> 00:13:09.980
like how did that transition happen?

00:13:09.980 --> 00:13:14.980
- Yeah, so I guess for me, I
never set out to write a book,

00:13:15.600 --> 00:13:17.540
like that was not on my mind.

00:13:17.540 --> 00:13:20.260
I set out to save my life.

00:13:20.260 --> 00:13:23.110
The origins of the book were,

00:13:23.110 --> 00:13:27.580
I grew up in a neglectful,
psychologically abusive household

00:13:27.580 --> 00:13:29.330
where things came to die.

00:13:29.330 --> 00:13:33.760
You know my first memory is
of a family of deer dying

00:13:33.760 --> 00:13:34.860
in our lap pool

00:13:34.860 --> 00:13:39.730
and me seeing the water a
lot, carcasses on a blue tarp.

00:13:39.730 --> 00:13:41.730
I was like probably five

00:13:41.730 --> 00:13:46.730
and it was just an
unsafe place to grow up,

00:13:47.000 --> 00:13:51.813
chaos, constant screaming from my parents.

00:13:53.327 --> 00:13:57.130
And I didn't have any role models of like,

00:13:57.130 --> 00:13:59.000
no one was taking care of me.

00:13:59.000 --> 00:14:00.720
They weren't taking care of them.

00:14:00.720 --> 00:14:03.630
There was just no thoughts

00:14:03.630 --> 00:14:07.620
that maybe it would be important
to take care of yourself.

00:14:07.620 --> 00:14:10.250
So I really used external achievement

00:14:10.250 --> 00:14:12.270
as a way to get myself out of that house.

00:14:12.270 --> 00:14:16.020
You know I was the teacher's
pet, doing the extra credit,

00:14:16.020 --> 00:14:20.410
extra curricular literary, magazine, APs,

00:14:20.410 --> 00:14:23.110
award, award, award, you
know, hustle, hustle, hustle.

00:14:24.290 --> 00:14:26.893
This will save me, like work will save me.

00:14:27.800 --> 00:14:29.500
And in a funny way it kinda did

00:14:29.500 --> 00:14:33.020
because it got me to Brown.

00:14:33.020 --> 00:14:35.380
You know which was like
a fancy Ivy League school

00:14:35.380 --> 00:14:37.490
that put like a nice stamp on me,

00:14:37.490 --> 00:14:40.450
which helped me get my
first entry-level job

00:14:40.450 --> 00:14:42.020
at Comedy Central.

00:14:42.020 --> 00:14:45.761
And I just kept hustling
and working and going

00:14:45.761 --> 00:14:47.440
'cause that's all I knew how to do.

00:14:47.440 --> 00:14:51.570
But in the inside, I
was utterly imploding.

00:14:51.570 --> 00:14:54.540
I was living in a permanent panic attack

00:14:54.540 --> 00:14:58.460
where my jaw would clench down so hard

00:14:58.460 --> 00:15:00.360
that like I understood

00:15:00.360 --> 00:15:03.340
what the term gnashing of teeth meant.

00:15:03.340 --> 00:15:06.730
Like it created like a chronic migraine,

00:15:06.730 --> 00:15:09.390
which then I had to take
other medication for

00:15:10.720 --> 00:15:15.050
and then I would self-medicate
with weed and booze and boys

00:15:15.050 --> 00:15:17.510
and just trying to distract

00:15:17.510 --> 00:15:19.750
because I had no interior life.

00:15:19.750 --> 00:15:22.230
Like there was no there, there,

00:15:22.230 --> 00:15:24.990
I didn't know who I was, I was lost.

00:15:24.990 --> 00:15:27.910
And I might've kept just going that way

00:15:27.910 --> 00:15:31.320
had I not hit rock bottom
on my 25th birthday

00:15:31.320 --> 00:15:33.520
when I drunk dialed my therapist

00:15:33.520 --> 00:15:35.123
and threatened to hurt myself.

00:15:36.130 --> 00:15:37.840
And for me that morning,

00:15:37.840 --> 00:15:41.050
the morning after when I
was listening to voicemails

00:15:41.050 --> 00:15:43.340
from her and kind of piecing together,

00:15:43.340 --> 00:15:48.340
oh my God, I'm in trouble, like
I could have killed myself.

00:15:48.350 --> 00:15:52.310
Like my therapist who
was like a very rational,

00:15:52.310 --> 00:15:55.920
calm, vaguely European woman

00:15:55.920 --> 00:15:59.270
who always just had on a
scarf and a little cup of tea,

00:15:59.270 --> 00:16:01.661
like she was worried

00:16:01.661 --> 00:16:04.200
and that was the first time I got worried.

00:16:04.200 --> 00:16:09.070
So that morning I just decided,
okay, I don't have parents.

00:16:09.070 --> 00:16:12.380
I don't have like a wise mentor to go to

00:16:12.380 --> 00:16:14.730
but I do have my hustle.

00:16:14.730 --> 00:16:17.397
Like I do have this thing in me

00:16:17.397 --> 00:16:19.680
that's always trying to achieve.

00:16:19.680 --> 00:16:24.680
So what if I made a Google Doc
of how to repair it myself?

00:16:25.030 --> 00:16:30.030
Like what if I tried and I
just wrote in the Google Doc,

00:16:30.220 --> 00:16:34.460
it started with just questions
I had like, what are values?

00:16:34.460 --> 00:16:35.720
What are principles?

00:16:35.720 --> 00:16:37.370
What are vegetables?

00:16:37.370 --> 00:16:41.290
Like genuinely what vegetables
should I be eating right now?

00:16:41.290 --> 00:16:42.350
What is quinoa?

00:16:42.350 --> 00:16:43.220
Is that a vegetable?

00:16:43.220 --> 00:16:44.403
The answer is no.

00:16:45.610 --> 00:16:49.273
Like so it was, it's an ancient grain.

00:16:50.260 --> 00:16:52.960
So it was really even that basic,

00:16:52.960 --> 00:16:55.580
like do I need to be drinking water?

00:16:55.580 --> 00:17:00.320
Yes, like would making
my bed be a good idea?

00:17:00.320 --> 00:17:03.020
Hell yes, like very basic things like that

00:17:03.020 --> 00:17:06.600
but then much bigger
things like confronting

00:17:06.600 --> 00:17:09.440
why am I in the same romantic relationship

00:17:09.440 --> 00:17:11.350
over and over and over again?

00:17:11.350 --> 00:17:13.720
And why am I so comfortable

00:17:13.720 --> 00:17:16.130
when other people make
me feel uncomfortable

00:17:16.130 --> 00:17:17.210
or make me feel small?

00:17:17.210 --> 00:17:19.397
Like why is that so familiar?

00:17:19.397 --> 00:17:22.590
And so I did this for five years

00:17:22.590 --> 00:17:24.040
and at the end of five years,

00:17:24.040 --> 00:17:26.830
I had a 600 page Google Document.

00:17:26.830 --> 00:17:29.020
I felt like a completely different person.

00:17:29.020 --> 00:17:31.410
I felt stable.

00:17:31.410 --> 00:17:33.270
You know I wasn't self-medicating,

00:17:33.270 --> 00:17:36.680
I wasn't running away from
my life, I was living it.

00:17:36.680 --> 00:17:40.660
And that's when I decided
I have an offering,

00:17:40.660 --> 00:17:42.950
I have something to
share with other people.

00:17:42.950 --> 00:17:46.380
I wish someone had shared
their journey with me

00:17:46.380 --> 00:17:48.430
that would have been helpful.

00:17:48.430 --> 00:17:51.110
And as I wrote that book

00:17:51.110 --> 00:17:55.180
and I realized, oh, I
have all these tricks too.

00:17:55.180 --> 00:17:57.950
'Cause just from my Google
Doc I would have a list,

00:17:57.950 --> 00:18:01.090
like I started listing
what makes me feel good.

00:18:01.090 --> 00:18:03.890
Like I really started noticing

00:18:03.890 --> 00:18:05.230
this is what makes me feel good

00:18:05.230 --> 00:18:08.420
and these are the questions
that helped me in my journal.

00:18:08.420 --> 00:18:13.420
And this is how exercising
affects my body and my brain.

00:18:14.730 --> 00:18:17.290
So I just had all of these lists

00:18:17.290 --> 00:18:19.460
that I thought might be
helpful to somebody else.

00:18:19.460 --> 00:18:24.460
And so they just were, became
a part of the stories to me.

00:18:25.620 --> 00:18:29.560
And once I realized it was useful,

00:18:29.560 --> 00:18:32.410
then I kind of got over my initial,

00:18:32.410 --> 00:18:33.890
uh, I don't wanna be a self-help writer.

00:18:33.890 --> 00:18:36.120
'Cause I'm like, wait, why?

00:18:36.120 --> 00:18:38.430
Every author who's ever lived,

00:18:38.430 --> 00:18:40.530
if their book made you feel less alone

00:18:40.530 --> 00:18:43.590
was a self-help author in some way,

00:18:43.590 --> 00:18:47.020
they reached out your
hand and said you belong

00:18:47.020 --> 00:18:48.550
and they do it in their own way.

00:18:48.550 --> 00:18:51.500
And I just wanted to be more explicit

00:18:51.500 --> 00:18:52.970
because I would have appreciated

00:18:52.970 --> 00:18:55.080
if someone had been more explicit with me.

00:18:55.080 --> 00:18:56.550
So that's sort of,

00:18:56.550 --> 00:18:58.670
I just wanted to be of use and of service.

00:18:58.670 --> 00:19:00.120
So that's how I got over

00:19:00.120 --> 00:19:03.040
the sort of my initial
distain of self-help.

00:19:03.040 --> 00:19:04.870
- Right, well, I appreciate you taking us,

00:19:04.870 --> 00:19:05.950
yeah, through that journey.

00:19:05.950 --> 00:19:08.296
I mean, there's so much there too.

00:19:08.296 --> 00:19:11.000
I'm just impressed by the
level of introspection

00:19:11.000 --> 00:19:11.890
that you had too,

00:19:11.890 --> 00:19:14.470
to be able to, and diligence
to go through that process.

00:19:14.470 --> 00:19:17.660
So I think a lot of us
will have just gotten tired

00:19:17.660 --> 00:19:19.520
of that process to be completely honest.

00:19:19.520 --> 00:19:21.253
And then like, I can't do this anymore.

00:19:21.253 --> 00:19:23.670
It's the fact that you're
able to kind of keep going,

00:19:23.670 --> 00:19:25.130
I mean, it sounds like you were seeing,

00:19:25.130 --> 00:19:27.010
would you say you were seeing
things change along the way

00:19:27.010 --> 00:19:29.043
or was it a specific moment?

00:19:30.220 --> 00:19:34.970
- Oh, every step was like a
brand new life was being born.

00:19:34.970 --> 00:19:39.500
And I really wanna be clear
that I wasn't pumped to do this.

00:19:39.500 --> 00:19:44.040
I wasn't like, yay, this is
gonna be so fun and not hard.

00:19:44.040 --> 00:19:47.740
I mean, there were many
times where I was just like,

00:19:47.740 --> 00:19:49.710
why do I have to work so hard at this?

00:19:49.710 --> 00:19:51.820
Like why do I, everybody else,

00:19:51.820 --> 00:19:54.200
like this is when I get really self-pity,

00:19:54.200 --> 00:19:55.560
which I definitely have in me.

00:19:55.560 --> 00:19:58.110
I'm like, why is everybody else
just get to live their life?

00:19:58.110 --> 00:20:00.640
And like here I am with
the stupid Google Document,

00:20:00.640 --> 00:20:04.810
like chatting how many drinks
of alcohol work for me.

00:20:04.810 --> 00:20:07.460
Like why am I even doing this?

00:20:07.460 --> 00:20:10.810
And why I was even doing this
was I wanted to be healthy.

00:20:10.810 --> 00:20:12.760
You know at the bottom of everything,

00:20:12.760 --> 00:20:14.993
it was like, well, it
was for my own health.

00:20:15.950 --> 00:20:19.260
And so I just kept going.

00:20:19.260 --> 00:20:23.600
And I think the thing
I was born with that,

00:20:23.600 --> 00:20:26.520
if you believe in God or
the universe or whatever,

00:20:26.520 --> 00:20:30.370
is I was born with a soul
that wanted to be healthy

00:20:30.370 --> 00:20:32.810
and I don't really have to
feed it much more than that.

00:20:32.810 --> 00:20:35.470
Like it just wants to be healthy.

00:20:35.470 --> 00:20:37.490
It just wants to be free.

00:20:37.490 --> 00:20:40.090
And so when I get tired of this stuff,

00:20:40.090 --> 00:20:42.630
I just lean back on the truth of me,

00:20:42.630 --> 00:20:47.210
which is I wanna be
liberated, I wanna be healthy

00:20:47.210 --> 00:20:49.670
and let that energy carry me.

00:20:49.670 --> 00:20:51.350
- Yeah, well, that's so thoughtful.

00:20:51.350 --> 00:20:53.770
- Was that to (indistinct),
did I just go way over?

00:20:53.770 --> 00:20:55.170
- That was definitely good.

00:20:55.170 --> 00:20:58.250
And I appreciate you talking
about the struggle too.

00:20:58.250 --> 00:21:01.159
'Cause I feel like that's half of it,

00:21:01.159 --> 00:21:03.500
like it doesn't help us when
we only hear about the victory

00:21:03.500 --> 00:21:04.333
on the other side.

00:21:04.333 --> 00:21:07.980
But to know that, like you're
like this is not exciting,

00:21:07.980 --> 00:21:10.120
it's painful but I've got to do it.

00:21:10.120 --> 00:21:13.210
And like you had this
internal God-given drive

00:21:13.210 --> 00:21:14.650
to just keep going.

00:21:14.650 --> 00:21:17.500
- I mean, the whole
preface to my second book

00:21:17.500 --> 00:21:19.220
which I'm working on right now,

00:21:19.220 --> 00:21:21.180
and it will be way cut down

00:21:21.180 --> 00:21:24.760
but is a, greatest hits of my complaints

00:21:24.760 --> 00:21:26.903
about why do I have to do this?

00:21:27.789 --> 00:21:32.070
It's never been like, ouh,
this is easy and so fun,

00:21:32.070 --> 00:21:34.030
like if you're listening to this.

00:21:34.030 --> 00:21:35.880
You know like, man, it's a struggle

00:21:35.880 --> 00:21:39.580
to face my life as it
really is and heal myself,

00:21:39.580 --> 00:21:41.230
you are correct.

00:21:41.230 --> 00:21:46.170
It is a struggle and it is
really hard and you can bear it

00:21:46.170 --> 00:21:47.780
because you already are.

00:21:47.780 --> 00:21:50.140
Whatever you've been through
is already dealing with you.

00:21:50.140 --> 00:21:51.830
So it's just an opportunity

00:21:51.830 --> 00:21:55.280
that you can choose your reaction.

00:21:55.280 --> 00:21:57.390
It's just like a slight shift,

00:21:57.390 --> 00:21:59.420
like it's a different kind of suffering.

00:21:59.420 --> 00:22:01.520
It's a suffering I enjoy more.

00:22:01.520 --> 00:22:04.200
- Yeah, that makes a lot of sense.

00:22:04.200 --> 00:22:05.710
I mean, in some ways as you're talking to,

00:22:05.710 --> 00:22:07.930
it seems like there's parallels too

00:22:07.930 --> 00:22:10.240
to the way that you approached
your work before that too.

00:22:10.240 --> 00:22:12.150
'Cause I mean, you basically
stayed on your grind.

00:22:12.150 --> 00:22:15.030
You made sure you knew what
you had to do, you did it.

00:22:15.030 --> 00:22:16.510
But with this it's the same type of thing

00:22:16.510 --> 00:22:18.820
and we don't always think about that way

00:22:18.820 --> 00:22:21.090
when we're dealing with
emotional challenges.

00:22:21.090 --> 00:22:22.370
Sometimes it's easy to just feel like,

00:22:22.370 --> 00:22:23.840
oh, I wish this was just fixed.

00:22:23.840 --> 00:22:26.170
Even though we know like
deep down I think we know

00:22:26.170 --> 00:22:27.890
we still got to put in that work

00:22:27.890 --> 00:22:29.750
but just doesn't come naturally.

00:22:29.750 --> 00:22:31.170
- You're exactly right.

00:22:31.170 --> 00:22:33.610
It's you know when we have a
work project and we're like,

00:22:33.610 --> 00:22:36.600
I need to, when we think about
it rationally, like goal,

00:22:36.600 --> 00:22:38.180
like here's the small goal than this

00:22:38.180 --> 00:22:40.570
and I'll lead to this and
that's how I'll achieve this.

00:22:40.570 --> 00:22:43.220
But then take a thing like generosity,

00:22:43.220 --> 00:22:45.310
like I wanna be more generous.

00:22:45.310 --> 00:22:49.220
We just are like, and magically
that will happen somehow

00:22:49.220 --> 00:22:51.710
or I wanna have a better
relationship with my mom

00:22:52.550 --> 00:22:55.400
and magically, like in
time, we'll do that.

00:22:55.400 --> 00:22:57.610
No, it's not time, it's work.

00:22:57.610 --> 00:23:00.540
Just like every other
thing in life, it's work,

00:23:00.540 --> 00:23:04.210
we can all do work so
we can all achieve this

00:23:04.210 --> 00:23:06.170
and that is the message of my book is.

00:23:06.170 --> 00:23:07.430
There is no special reason

00:23:07.430 --> 00:23:10.203
that I should have been
able to repair it myself.

00:23:12.150 --> 00:23:15.480
I'm special in certain ways,
that's not how I'm special.

00:23:15.480 --> 00:23:19.000
It was just sticking to doing work

00:23:19.000 --> 00:23:24.000
and having a little
faith that I was worth it

00:23:24.060 --> 00:23:25.450
and that I could do it.

00:23:25.450 --> 00:23:27.660
- Yeah, I think that's so good

00:23:27.660 --> 00:23:28.980
and you're reaping the benefits

00:23:28.980 --> 00:23:31.390
and then helping so many
people at the same time.

00:23:31.390 --> 00:23:34.380
Again, it was such a process,
it sounds like to get there.

00:23:34.380 --> 00:23:36.070
'Cause I even heard you talk about

00:23:36.070 --> 00:23:37.810
ways that you felt like
you're really good at work

00:23:37.810 --> 00:23:38.923
but bad at life.

00:23:39.850 --> 00:23:42.960
You had to really come to terms with that.

00:23:42.960 --> 00:23:44.960
And again, it goes back to that whole idea

00:23:44.960 --> 00:23:46.550
of just being honest with ourselves

00:23:46.550 --> 00:23:49.710
and be like, okay, this
aspect of life needs work

00:23:49.710 --> 00:23:51.180
and I can't just leave it

00:23:51.180 --> 00:23:55.000
and expect it to all
of a sudden fix itself.

00:23:55.000 --> 00:23:57.650
- It's honesty and self-awareness.

00:23:57.650 --> 00:24:02.020
Those are the keys to having a life

00:24:02.020 --> 00:24:04.673
that doesn't feel like
it's happening to you.

00:24:06.110 --> 00:24:09.520
It's, and it takes a
lot to look at yourself

00:24:09.520 --> 00:24:10.980
and all of your flaws and say like,

00:24:10.980 --> 00:24:13.103
well, this just is really me.

00:24:14.380 --> 00:24:18.890
But what I've found is, in
getting back to acceptance,

00:24:18.890 --> 00:24:22.350
just accepting that
gives me so many options.

00:24:22.350 --> 00:24:25.760
I have options now to change if I want to

00:24:25.760 --> 00:24:27.780
and there are some things
about me I don't wanna change,

00:24:27.780 --> 00:24:31.190
like I'm an extremely trusting person

00:24:31.190 --> 00:24:33.240
often to my own detriment.

00:24:33.240 --> 00:24:35.510
I don't want to change that about me.

00:24:35.510 --> 00:24:36.800
Like I like that,

00:24:36.800 --> 00:24:40.580
I like that 'cause it opens
me up to a lot of possibility

00:24:40.580 --> 00:24:42.090
and it's painful

00:24:42.090 --> 00:24:46.750
because that's living with
your heart on your shoulders,

00:24:46.750 --> 00:24:49.020
just can be a little painful.

00:24:49.020 --> 00:24:52.160
And I can handle that,

00:24:52.160 --> 00:24:55.323
well, you know what,
I'm doing it currently.

00:24:56.180 --> 00:24:57.890
So I do think it's

00:24:57.890 --> 00:25:00.590
that honest accounting
of where we really are

00:25:00.590 --> 00:25:02.910
and being self-aware enough to know,

00:25:02.910 --> 00:25:04.900
and I can change this with some work.

00:25:04.900 --> 00:25:08.200
Like it's not just gonna
magically hit one day.

00:25:08.200 --> 00:25:10.200
- Yeah, I think that's so well said.

00:25:10.200 --> 00:25:11.360
I'm also curious 'cause
there's so many (indistinct)

00:25:11.360 --> 00:25:13.500
I feel like resonate with a lot of people,

00:25:13.500 --> 00:25:16.310
just how people have
responded to the book itself.

00:25:16.310 --> 00:25:17.143
'Cause one of the pieces

00:25:17.143 --> 00:25:18.970
that I feel like you've
highlighted really well

00:25:18.970 --> 00:25:23.460
is just how easy it is to be
dismissive of any challenges.

00:25:23.460 --> 00:25:27.310
And just to think, well,
so-and-so has it worse than me,

00:25:27.310 --> 00:25:28.390
which could be true.

00:25:28.390 --> 00:25:32.040
It's often true and kind
of look back at ourselves

00:25:32.040 --> 00:25:33.270
and be like, oh, I
shouldn't be struggling.

00:25:33.270 --> 00:25:36.290
'Cause I mean, I've seen that
a lot during the pandemic.

00:25:36.290 --> 00:25:38.430
Like any specific
individual that I talked to,

00:25:38.430 --> 00:25:40.360
whatever their circumstances,

00:25:40.360 --> 00:25:41.810
they feel bad that they're struggling

00:25:41.810 --> 00:25:43.760
because they can pick out
someone else who's doing worse

00:25:43.760 --> 00:25:46.050
and they feel like why, I
shouldn't be struggling.

00:25:46.050 --> 00:25:47.930
They are struggling, so
they can't get as much done

00:25:47.930 --> 00:25:48.990
or they're struggling in certain areas

00:25:48.990 --> 00:25:50.100
and they feel anxious and depressed

00:25:50.100 --> 00:25:51.660
'cause they can't move to that.

00:25:51.660 --> 00:25:53.070
Maybe not clinical depression per se,

00:25:53.070 --> 00:25:57.000
but that loophole definitely
starts to kick in pretty early.

00:25:57.000 --> 00:25:59.920
So I'm curious if you had people
talked with you about that

00:25:59.920 --> 00:26:01.180
as they've been reading
through your books?

00:26:01.180 --> 00:26:03.010
Are there ways that
you've tried to even move

00:26:03.010 --> 00:26:05.420
past that kind of
reaction in your own life?

00:26:05.420 --> 00:26:07.240
- Yeah, I mean, absolutely.

00:26:07.240 --> 00:26:08.870
And you're hitting on

00:26:08.870 --> 00:26:12.230
I think one of the biggest
things that I've found.

00:26:12.230 --> 00:26:14.330
'Cause for so long I didn't wanna heal

00:26:14.330 --> 00:26:17.240
because I didn't have it that bad.

00:26:17.240 --> 00:26:20.980
I should be better, and I'm
doing massive air quotes.

00:26:20.980 --> 00:26:22.470
And what I kind of realized

00:26:22.470 --> 00:26:27.280
was that was not
beneficial for other people

00:26:27.280 --> 00:26:29.280
who perhaps were suffering more from me,

00:26:29.280 --> 00:26:32.700
like me minimizing my
experience it didn't help them.

00:26:32.700 --> 00:26:35.500
And it certainly wasn't helping me,

00:26:35.500 --> 00:26:40.190
so just who in the world
was I dismissing myself for?

00:26:40.190 --> 00:26:43.140
And once I kind of like
saw that kind of clearly,

00:26:43.140 --> 00:26:46.850
I realized this isn't helpful,
this isn't productive,

00:26:46.850 --> 00:26:48.610
I gotta reframe this.

00:26:48.610 --> 00:26:51.210
And the writer Lori Gottlieb,

00:26:51.210 --> 00:26:52.900
who wrote, Maybe You
Should Talk to Someone.

00:26:52.900 --> 00:26:55.090
And she's one of my absolute favorite

00:26:55.090 --> 00:26:57.930
writers, thinkers, everything.

00:26:57.930 --> 00:26:59.390
She talks about in her book

00:26:59.390 --> 00:27:03.410
this theory of, there's
no hierarchy of pain.

00:27:03.410 --> 00:27:04.920
There really isn't,

00:27:04.920 --> 00:27:09.920
nobody's pain is any more
valid than anyone else's

00:27:10.050 --> 00:27:11.483
because it's real.

00:27:12.810 --> 00:27:14.630
Like you're talking about your friends

00:27:14.630 --> 00:27:17.910
or someone who they're
denying their own suffering

00:27:17.910 --> 00:27:20.130
because it's not a worst case scenario.

00:27:20.130 --> 00:27:23.430
That doesn't mean it isn't
still painful for them

00:27:23.430 --> 00:27:26.320
and that isn't their absolute reality.

00:27:26.320 --> 00:27:28.970
And so again, if we deny what is real

00:27:28.970 --> 00:27:30.663
then we can't deal with anything.

00:27:32.310 --> 00:27:36.570
I often ask myself, what would
the wisest version of me,

00:27:36.570 --> 00:27:40.120
how would the wisest version
of me handled this situation?

00:27:40.120 --> 00:27:41.710
I call it future Tara.

00:27:41.710 --> 00:27:44.613
Like how would future Tara handle this?

00:27:45.630 --> 00:27:48.130
'Cause I don't have all this wisdom now,

00:27:48.130 --> 00:27:49.830
like in the future maybe,

00:27:49.830 --> 00:27:51.810
so like how would she deal with this?

00:27:51.810 --> 00:27:55.790
And often it's, I wouldn't dismiss myself

00:27:55.790 --> 00:27:57.800
or wouldn't call myself stupid and silly.

00:27:57.800 --> 00:28:00.690
I wouldn't demean myself.

00:28:00.690 --> 00:28:03.650
I would look at the actual circumstances

00:28:03.650 --> 00:28:07.260
and try to help myself,
try to resource myself

00:28:07.260 --> 00:28:10.490
and deal with them so that
they did not deal with me.

00:28:10.490 --> 00:28:12.700
And so readers have
really picked up on this

00:28:12.700 --> 00:28:15.300
and they've talked a
lot about feeling like,

00:28:15.300 --> 00:28:18.630
they didn't have, the quote
unquote, worst childhood ever.

00:28:18.630 --> 00:28:23.410
And that because it wasn't
on the news level bad,

00:28:23.410 --> 00:28:24.860
they never dealt with it.

00:28:24.860 --> 00:28:27.180
But now they see that
it's dealing with them

00:28:27.180 --> 00:28:30.420
and that they have to
go back and do some work

00:28:30.420 --> 00:28:32.450
and confront these things

00:28:32.450 --> 00:28:35.820
that they actually are
confronting every single day.

00:28:35.820 --> 00:28:39.403
So yeah, I've really seen
that people kind of being,

00:28:40.400 --> 00:28:44.520
pausing and denying
their actual experience.

00:28:44.520 --> 00:28:45.833
- Yeah, and I think that's great

00:28:45.833 --> 00:28:47.233
to just to get people to realize that

00:28:47.233 --> 00:28:51.110
because that's half, sounded
like GI Joe, half the battle

00:28:51.110 --> 00:28:52.960
but I'm also over age.

00:28:52.960 --> 00:28:54.860
- No, it isn't, it's also like,

00:28:54.860 --> 00:28:57.100
we're still having trouble with it.

00:28:57.100 --> 00:28:58.860
Like it's also condescending

00:28:58.860 --> 00:29:02.340
to think of other people
as much, much worse.

00:29:02.340 --> 00:29:04.060
Like who are you even,

00:29:04.060 --> 00:29:07.200
like the whole thing is
so backwards and weird

00:29:07.200 --> 00:29:09.070
that it's like better if you can just,

00:29:09.070 --> 00:29:10.863
however you need to move off of it.

00:29:10.863 --> 00:29:13.220
Just like let's just
move off of that idea.

00:29:13.220 --> 00:29:15.480
- Yeah, and even the way that you're,

00:29:15.480 --> 00:29:17.770
I mean, the way that you're
inspiring so many people to,

00:29:17.770 --> 00:29:19.469
I mean, another topic
that we talk about a lot

00:29:19.469 --> 00:29:22.220
and I know we talked about
this offline before too.

00:29:22.220 --> 00:29:24.340
Just that whole balance between

00:29:24.340 --> 00:29:27.310
helping yourself versus
helping other people

00:29:27.310 --> 00:29:31.350
and just the danger of
ignoring our own challenges

00:29:31.350 --> 00:29:32.590
and trying to help other people.

00:29:32.590 --> 00:29:35.390
I mean, that's also come
up in other podcasts before

00:29:35.390 --> 00:29:36.870
with other psychologists
that have come on,

00:29:36.870 --> 00:29:39.270
you know as us being healers,
the healers can't help heal

00:29:39.270 --> 00:29:41.390
if they're not going
through that healing too.

00:29:41.390 --> 00:29:43.960
So how do you balance
that in your own life?

00:29:43.960 --> 00:29:46.440
I mean, with this explosion of support

00:29:46.440 --> 00:29:49.130
that you're giving while
you still have your own life

00:29:49.130 --> 00:29:50.841
to kind of walk through too?

00:29:50.841 --> 00:29:55.841
- It's been an unexpected
little bit of an adjustment

00:29:56.320 --> 00:30:01.320
because I don't, 'cause
I'm still in process.

00:30:01.730 --> 00:30:04.400
And the thing about my book and my writing

00:30:04.400 --> 00:30:06.560
is like I don't know all the answers.

00:30:06.560 --> 00:30:07.950
I'm not an expert.

00:30:07.950 --> 00:30:10.100
Like I don't have a single degree

00:30:10.100 --> 00:30:11.880
in any of the things I'm talking about.

00:30:11.880 --> 00:30:14.770
What I am is a little sponge.

00:30:14.770 --> 00:30:18.210
Like I just sponge in
everything that I can learn

00:30:18.210 --> 00:30:20.930
and then I try to apply it in a real way

00:30:20.930 --> 00:30:23.430
that I think is relatable to other people

00:30:23.430 --> 00:30:24.660
'cause I can really break it down.

00:30:24.660 --> 00:30:26.380
So that's what I am.

00:30:26.380 --> 00:30:27.970
I'm not the one who did the research,

00:30:27.970 --> 00:30:31.383
I'm not the one who like has
your degree and is at Yale.

00:30:34.110 --> 00:30:37.670
So I've really had to
kind of say to myself is,

00:30:37.670 --> 00:30:38.503
and I don't know,

00:30:38.503 --> 00:30:41.020
I'm trying to still
really figure it out is,

00:30:41.020 --> 00:30:43.670
how can I be the most helpful?

00:30:43.670 --> 00:30:45.807
I know what feels the best,

00:30:45.807 --> 00:30:50.270
the best feelings I have are
when I'm writing the book.

00:30:50.270 --> 00:30:53.560
Like when I'm really,
really trying to be honest

00:30:53.560 --> 00:30:57.050
and the work connects with people.

00:30:57.050 --> 00:31:00.773
I like when I speak to a bigger crowd,

00:31:01.610 --> 00:31:03.370
that feels really good.

00:31:03.370 --> 00:31:07.100
I like when I talk
one-on-one with somebody

00:31:07.100 --> 00:31:09.200
if I just like meet them,

00:31:09.200 --> 00:31:12.030
but constantly being in my DMs

00:31:12.030 --> 00:31:14.640
or replying to every single email,

00:31:14.640 --> 00:31:18.310
even though these messages
are utterly beautiful

00:31:18.310 --> 00:31:19.143
and even though people

00:31:19.143 --> 00:31:23.410
are gracing me with the
hardest moments of their lives,

00:31:23.410 --> 00:31:25.670
if I do every single one

00:31:25.670 --> 00:31:30.460
I can't write, I can't go
speak to a bigger group.

00:31:30.460 --> 00:31:35.460
So I myself, because I'm
so, I crave connection,

00:31:36.730 --> 00:31:40.600
given that I had none growing
up and I felt so alone.

00:31:40.600 --> 00:31:44.690
Like one of my driving forces
is a feeling of connection.

00:31:44.690 --> 00:31:48.120
And so I'm having to figure
out how do I connect?

00:31:48.120 --> 00:31:53.120
How do I help in a way that
I am not completely depleted

00:31:53.280 --> 00:31:55.283
and so that I can continue the work?

00:31:56.220 --> 00:31:58.230
So it's really like an open question.

00:31:58.230 --> 00:32:03.230
I don't know the answer,
gen I have no idea.

00:32:04.390 --> 00:32:06.810
I'm just still trying to kind
of figure out for myself.

00:32:06.810 --> 00:32:08.260
- It's a process and a journey.

00:32:08.260 --> 00:32:09.360
I mean, if I'm being completely honest

00:32:09.360 --> 00:32:11.620
I feel like I'm on that
journey in some ways too.

00:32:11.620 --> 00:32:14.090
- And you must be in particular,

00:32:14.090 --> 00:32:15.940
'cause we talked a little bit offline too

00:32:15.940 --> 00:32:18.590
about how even in your profession,

00:32:18.590 --> 00:32:21.870
like professionally your job is to help.

00:32:21.870 --> 00:32:25.930
But then does it become
everyone coming to you

00:32:25.930 --> 00:32:30.930
to kind of like fix or explain
to them what's going on.

00:32:31.010 --> 00:32:33.550
And does that feel like such a response?

00:32:33.550 --> 00:32:37.350
Like I could imagine that
feeling both like an honor,

00:32:37.350 --> 00:32:38.860
you know this person's coming to me

00:32:38.860 --> 00:32:40.860
but also wait, wait, wait,

00:32:40.860 --> 00:32:43.270
like I need to maintain some boundaries.

00:32:43.270 --> 00:32:45.570
- Yeah, so I think even for me

00:32:45.570 --> 00:32:47.400
it's something I'm still walking through

00:32:47.400 --> 00:32:49.950
and figuring out on a day-to-day basis.

00:32:49.950 --> 00:32:50.830
Because like you said,

00:32:50.830 --> 00:32:54.930
each of those conversations
can be so personal

00:32:54.930 --> 00:32:59.490
and touching and
empowering in a sense too.

00:32:59.490 --> 00:33:02.760
But then knowing how
much to engage in those

00:33:02.760 --> 00:33:04.940
versus there's a lot
of research that we do,

00:33:04.940 --> 00:33:07.390
there's this podcast obviously.

00:33:07.390 --> 00:33:09.360
And so you know I still
have to eat and sleep

00:33:09.360 --> 00:33:11.400
but just like how do you balance that

00:33:11.400 --> 00:33:13.650
and not spread yourself so thin

00:33:13.650 --> 00:33:15.540
that you become completely
ineffective for everyone?

00:33:15.540 --> 00:33:17.050
So I appreciate the
way you're sharing that

00:33:17.050 --> 00:33:18.470
and I'd say a lot of ways.

00:33:18.470 --> 00:33:20.330
Yeah, I wouldn't say I've
arrived in that place

00:33:20.330 --> 00:33:23.630
but some of it is just having
folks who can look into,

00:33:23.630 --> 00:33:24.670
and kind of watch and say,

00:33:24.670 --> 00:33:27.010
okay, I think you're getting
a little bit spread thin here,

00:33:27.010 --> 00:33:28.740
is there anything to go back on?

00:33:28.740 --> 00:33:31.730
- That's interesting,
can I ask you a question?

00:33:31.730 --> 00:33:33.850
- Yeah, flip the script.

00:33:33.850 --> 00:33:35.620
- Yeah, I gotta flip the
script for the second.

00:33:35.620 --> 00:33:38.177
Who in your life asks you that question

00:33:38.177 --> 00:33:41.330
like, "Hey, are you spread too thin?"

00:33:41.330 --> 00:33:42.840
- Yeah, so I'd say some of it

00:33:42.840 --> 00:33:45.530
is just like going to
people in faith communities.

00:33:45.530 --> 00:33:47.030
So a pastor that I'm pretty close with

00:33:47.030 --> 00:33:49.570
who has basically been in this journey.

00:33:49.570 --> 00:33:51.470
We did an event together back in 2019

00:33:51.470 --> 00:33:54.190
called God's mental health
and wellness in New York City.

00:33:54.190 --> 00:33:56.960
So having him just kind
of interject and check in

00:33:56.960 --> 00:33:58.800
and see how things are
going, opportunities

00:33:58.800 --> 00:34:02.360
and just sometimes not even
having to ask the question

00:34:02.360 --> 00:34:05.260
but knowing me well enough
to be able to kind of sense

00:34:05.260 --> 00:34:08.520
and ask questions that
will get me to that place.

00:34:08.520 --> 00:34:10.420
And in sometimes to be honest it's just,

00:34:10.420 --> 00:34:13.230
even just acquaintances
or, not even acquaintances

00:34:13.230 --> 00:34:15.330
but colleagues that I know really well,

00:34:15.330 --> 00:34:16.530
when we sit down and talk about

00:34:16.530 --> 00:34:17.900
all the different initiatives

00:34:17.900 --> 00:34:19.510
that are going on in the research,

00:34:19.510 --> 00:34:20.510
I'm also doing a lot of work

00:34:20.510 --> 00:34:23.230
around diversity, equity
and inclusion, anti-racism,

00:34:23.230 --> 00:34:25.970
there's a podcast, there's
informal mentoring.

00:34:25.970 --> 00:34:27.430
So people were asking are you taking,

00:34:27.430 --> 00:34:28.670
I mean, someone asked me last week,

00:34:28.670 --> 00:34:31.270
are you taking enough time for yourself?

00:34:31.270 --> 00:34:32.114
Like are you actually

00:34:32.114 --> 00:34:33.650
setting back?
- What was your answer?

00:34:33.650 --> 00:34:35.380
- I said it's a strategic process.

00:34:35.380 --> 00:34:36.680
Some days are better than the others.

00:34:36.680 --> 00:34:37.792
And then you know you're balancing,

00:34:37.792 --> 00:34:40.130
I mean, I'm married, I have kids as well.

00:34:40.130 --> 00:34:42.140
So you put all that in the mix.

00:34:42.140 --> 00:34:43.630
So to be honest, like to me,

00:34:43.630 --> 00:34:46.130
when someone asks that
kind of point of question,

00:34:46.130 --> 00:34:50.238
it's challenging, frustrating
and refreshing all the time.

00:34:50.238 --> 00:34:52.230
Because that's really when
I have the opportunity

00:34:52.230 --> 00:34:56.160
to check myself and say,
"Well, is this actually true?"

00:34:56.160 --> 00:34:57.430
Or am I getting to a point

00:34:57.430 --> 00:34:59.770
where this is just not sustainable, so?

00:34:59.770 --> 00:35:01.980
- Well, it's so important
to have people in your life

00:35:01.980 --> 00:35:06.760
who like truly know you in that
way to even ask the question

00:35:06.760 --> 00:35:10.513
and that they're not afraid
to ask like, is this too much?

00:35:11.590 --> 00:35:13.610
I often silence those people.

00:35:13.610 --> 00:35:17.170
You know, I have a couple of
people in my life were like,

00:35:17.170 --> 00:35:19.160
you're doing what now and
what now and what now,

00:35:19.160 --> 00:35:19.993
is that too much?

00:35:19.993 --> 00:35:23.803
I'm Like, I can handle it,
like don't put me down.

00:35:24.700 --> 00:35:28.820
But when I can be not
defensive and not a jerk

00:35:28.820 --> 00:35:30.540
and hear the question,

00:35:30.540 --> 00:35:33.330
it's always, yeah, you're
doing way too much.

00:35:33.330 --> 00:35:37.890
Like you need to find a
middle gear and I accept that,

00:35:37.890 --> 00:35:41.100
except I'm, this is a
place where I'm processing

00:35:41.100 --> 00:35:44.003
and trying to figure out
like, what's my middle gear.

00:35:45.440 --> 00:35:47.130
- Well, I appreciate you asking honestly

00:35:47.130 --> 00:35:48.990
and asking the question too.

00:35:48.990 --> 00:35:50.140
- Yeah.
- I was gonna get there

00:35:50.140 --> 00:35:50.973
but you may be pulled out

00:35:50.973 --> 00:35:52.180
even more than that I probably would've

00:35:52.180 --> 00:35:54.920
so you're very skilled in that sense.

00:35:54.920 --> 00:35:55.890
- My trick.

00:35:55.890 --> 00:35:59.320
- Yeah, I think it's so
important in a lot of ways

00:35:59.320 --> 00:36:01.460
'cause it's so easy for us to just,

00:36:01.460 --> 00:36:06.010
in several ways just spread
too thin and not be about,

00:36:06.010 --> 00:36:08.638
almost like we're being
about too much at once.

00:36:08.638 --> 00:36:12.360
And I've talked to some people
just in terms of even just

00:36:12.360 --> 00:36:14.050
professionally people are saying,

00:36:14.050 --> 00:36:16.870
well, you know you're gonna
have to say no sometimes

00:36:16.870 --> 00:36:19.550
and you're gonna feel guilty about that

00:36:19.550 --> 00:36:22.245
but you actually just kind
of have to deal with it

00:36:22.245 --> 00:36:23.213
and ride the guilt

00:36:24.337 --> 00:36:26.680
and just think about
where your priorities are.

00:36:26.680 --> 00:36:28.520
So I think that's another place that,

00:36:28.520 --> 00:36:29.610
it takes some time to get there.

00:36:29.610 --> 00:36:33.750
- And I think the pandemic
has kind of hastened the idea

00:36:33.750 --> 00:36:38.170
that we need to stick to our priorities.

00:36:38.170 --> 00:36:40.490
You know I think a lot of
people what they saw is,

00:36:40.490 --> 00:36:43.500
you know maybe going to like 50 weddings

00:36:43.500 --> 00:36:48.460
and constantly out of town
and work, work, work, work,

00:36:48.460 --> 00:36:53.460
wasn't making me happy and was
it like weird own priority?

00:36:55.360 --> 00:36:59.560
And isn't actually in line with
what my real priorities are,

00:36:59.560 --> 00:37:00.550
or for a lot of people

00:37:00.550 --> 00:37:02.100
it was the first time they ask themselves

00:37:02.100 --> 00:37:04.350
what are my priorities?

00:37:04.350 --> 00:37:06.010
What are they even?

00:37:06.010 --> 00:37:09.520
And I really hope that
coming out of the pandemic,

00:37:09.520 --> 00:37:14.520
we can retain some sense of
our priorities of who we are

00:37:16.040 --> 00:37:19.990
and how we want to carry
ourselves in the world

00:37:20.940 --> 00:37:23.460
and then follow through.

00:37:23.460 --> 00:37:26.700
So, I remember before the pandemic

00:37:26.700 --> 00:37:28.410
like always being on a work trip,

00:37:28.410 --> 00:37:32.460
complaining to my friends
about how much I had to travel.

00:37:32.460 --> 00:37:35.000
And now I look back and like, what a jerk?

00:37:35.000 --> 00:37:38.870
Like wasn't even looking at it
as the gratitude for a while,

00:37:38.870 --> 00:37:40.390
I get to go to cool places,

00:37:40.390 --> 00:37:43.970
was so jaded that I'm like complaining

00:37:43.970 --> 00:37:45.210
and who did that benefit?

00:37:45.210 --> 00:37:47.290
Because I wasn't enjoying it.

00:37:47.290 --> 00:37:52.190
And the people I was flying to
their bachelorette into loom

00:37:52.190 --> 00:37:55.390
or whatever the detail was,

00:37:55.390 --> 00:37:58.960
you know people know when
you don't wanna be somewhere.

00:37:58.960 --> 00:38:01.070
It's not like you're fooling everybody.

00:38:01.070 --> 00:38:04.903
Like we're all smart, like we know.

00:38:06.400 --> 00:38:09.460
The woman who you're like
have grudgingly agreed

00:38:09.460 --> 00:38:10.360
to go to lunch with,

00:38:10.360 --> 00:38:14.793
like as you check your watch,
she doesn't feel like seen.

00:38:15.890 --> 00:38:20.217
So even with things like,
people are starting to ask

00:38:20.217 --> 00:38:22.710
can you go to coffee again?

00:38:22.710 --> 00:38:24.640
'Cause you know, I'm vaccinated,

00:38:24.640 --> 00:38:26.560
you know other afraid vaccinated people.

00:38:26.560 --> 00:38:30.350
And I'm like, do I really
wanna go to coffee?

00:38:30.350 --> 00:38:33.403
'Cause I don't think I do, so now what?

00:38:34.370 --> 00:38:37.370
I'm really like, I'm just in real time,

00:38:37.370 --> 00:38:40.860
stating my issues right now.

00:38:40.860 --> 00:38:43.957
- So in some ways it sounds
like you're reprioritizing too.

00:38:43.957 --> 00:38:45.640
And I think a lot of
people have done that,

00:38:45.640 --> 00:38:48.250
especially the way you
talked about your trips too.

00:38:48.250 --> 00:38:49.610
- Yeah, definitely.

00:38:49.610 --> 00:38:50.757
- I have to do this after this

00:38:50.757 --> 00:38:52.480
and I know you've talked
about that before too,

00:38:52.480 --> 00:38:54.203
shifting to I get to do this.

00:38:55.040 --> 00:38:56.330
I mean, I think for even me personally,

00:38:56.330 --> 00:38:59.110
a lot of us those trips
now feel like a luxury

00:38:59.110 --> 00:39:01.410
like, oh, if I were
gonna do that right now,

00:39:01.410 --> 00:39:04.000
just a whole different
perspective on things.

00:39:04.000 --> 00:39:08.283
- I'm kind of scared to be
honest, I haven't flown yet.

00:39:09.986 --> 00:39:12.650
I don't have any real
like big trips planned

00:39:14.161 --> 00:39:18.423
and I used to travel all of
the time and I love traveling.

00:39:19.480 --> 00:39:21.010
And now I'm just kind of scared,

00:39:21.010 --> 00:39:24.390
I'm scared of my life
getting out of control.

00:39:24.390 --> 00:39:26.930
I don't want to ever go back to the place

00:39:26.930 --> 00:39:29.610
where I wasn't prioritizing

00:39:29.610 --> 00:39:31.550
the things that really mattered to me.

00:39:31.550 --> 00:39:35.330
And the pandemic, I was
fortunate to be in a situation

00:39:35.330 --> 00:39:38.593
where I got to focus on the
things that mattered to me.

00:39:39.490 --> 00:39:44.490
And it's almost like a drug
addict and like I'm seeing like,

00:39:45.060 --> 00:39:46.370
and now I'm in the room again

00:39:46.370 --> 00:39:50.490
with all the things that used
to get me high or whatever.

00:39:50.490 --> 00:39:52.670
And then for me it's
like travel, distraction,

00:39:52.670 --> 00:39:57.530
constant plans, planning,
doing, that's my drug of choice.

00:40:00.090 --> 00:40:01.500
And so I'm a little afraid,

00:40:01.500 --> 00:40:03.300
like I'm actually a little afraid.

00:40:03.300 --> 00:40:05.710
- Yeah, how are you going to re-engage?

00:40:05.710 --> 00:40:07.170
Do you have, between that

00:40:07.170 --> 00:40:08.820
and just like your daily self-care,

00:40:08.820 --> 00:40:12.450
like how do you plan
to step into the world

00:40:12.450 --> 00:40:14.050
or is that still a process
you're figuring out?

00:40:14.050 --> 00:40:19.050
- I think it's say, no,
more, like doing a lot less,

00:40:20.060 --> 00:40:25.060
doing the things I actually
want to do and baby stepping.

00:40:26.300 --> 00:40:29.840
I'm gonna take my first trip
with two of my best friends.

00:40:29.840 --> 00:40:31.990
We're gonna do it one overnight,

00:40:31.990 --> 00:40:36.030
one night overnight by
the beach this weekend.

00:40:36.030 --> 00:40:38.033
You know that's feels like a good,

00:40:39.030 --> 00:40:41.210
start small, start incrementally,

00:40:41.210 --> 00:40:43.040
remember the big picture which is,

00:40:43.040 --> 00:40:44.980
I don't wanna over plan again.

00:40:44.980 --> 00:40:48.590
I don't need to distract
myself from my life,

00:40:48.590 --> 00:40:50.733
not everything has to be an achievement.

00:40:51.760 --> 00:40:54.560
And even working it into
my everyday schedule

00:40:54.560 --> 00:40:57.240
so that I have more breaks,

00:40:57.240 --> 00:40:59.500
you know even when I'm writing

00:40:59.500 --> 00:41:02.470
that I write by timing myself.

00:41:02.470 --> 00:41:03.680
So I have like a timer

00:41:03.680 --> 00:41:07.583
and when the timer goes off
after an hour, I'm free.

00:41:08.550 --> 00:41:11.410
So then I get up, I dance to one song,

00:41:11.410 --> 00:41:13.830
then I sit back down, it's my second hour

00:41:13.830 --> 00:41:16.120
but it's that little break,

00:41:16.120 --> 00:41:20.950
you know that little time
to enjoy and not to produce

00:41:20.950 --> 00:41:22.423
and not make anything.

00:41:23.780 --> 00:41:27.860
So I'm trying to, I'm really
trying to bake into my life

00:41:27.860 --> 00:41:31.260
the habits of what my
actual priorities are

00:41:31.260 --> 00:41:36.260
as opposed to just get back
into the swing of things

00:41:36.380 --> 00:41:38.783
as soon as I possibly can.

00:41:40.310 --> 00:41:42.150
- Yeah, I appreciate you kind
of walking us through that

00:41:42.150 --> 00:41:42.983
in real time.

00:41:42.983 --> 00:41:43.880
And I think that's so,

00:41:43.880 --> 00:41:46.350
I mean, I love the just
the incremental steps,

00:41:46.350 --> 00:41:47.780
it's just kind of re-engaging.

00:41:47.780 --> 00:41:48.901
I mean, in a lot of ways,

00:41:48.901 --> 00:41:50.690
like everything is talking
about is self-care too.

00:41:50.690 --> 00:41:53.190
And thinking ahead in some ways

00:41:53.190 --> 00:41:54.930
when you're talking about future Tara,

00:41:54.930 --> 00:41:57.520
it's like you're anticipating
what you'll learn

00:41:57.520 --> 00:41:59.583
and trying to teach that to yourself now.

00:41:59.583 --> 00:42:01.579
So you can to continue to move forward

00:42:01.579 --> 00:42:03.120
which I think is great.

00:42:03.120 --> 00:42:05.480
- I think it's really to give future Tara

00:42:05.480 --> 00:42:09.450
the resources she needs
'cause I'm not smart enough.

00:42:09.450 --> 00:42:13.800
I don't know like what she's gonna learn,

00:42:13.800 --> 00:42:15.040
I'm not a fortune teller

00:42:15.040 --> 00:42:19.470
but like I can fill out what resources

00:42:19.470 --> 00:42:22.910
that person I might want to be needs

00:42:24.416 --> 00:42:26.440
and I can give her that.

00:42:26.440 --> 00:42:28.730
I can make choices today you know,

00:42:28.730 --> 00:42:31.080
and that is self-care

00:42:31.080 --> 00:42:32.120
because that's everything.

00:42:32.120 --> 00:42:34.243
That's like, so I can go for a run,

00:42:35.312 --> 00:42:36.450
I don't know ultimately,

00:42:36.450 --> 00:42:38.010
am I gonna lose a little weight, am I not?

00:42:38.010 --> 00:42:38.843
I don't know.

00:42:38.843 --> 00:42:43.440
But I can go on this
run, that's how I eat,

00:42:43.440 --> 00:42:45.310
what's this all gonna mean in the future?

00:42:45.310 --> 00:42:46.143
I don't know.

00:42:46.143 --> 00:42:48.600
But I can eat more fruits and vegetables,

00:42:48.600 --> 00:42:50.280
like I definitely can do that.

00:42:50.280 --> 00:42:53.010
And it's even like very practical things

00:42:53.010 --> 00:42:55.610
like around fertility,

00:42:55.610 --> 00:42:58.130
like I just went through
an egg retrieval process,

00:42:58.130 --> 00:43:00.660
which was a whole other topic.

00:43:00.660 --> 00:43:03.740
But I definitely did that for
the future version of myself

00:43:03.740 --> 00:43:05.453
to have more options.

00:43:06.600 --> 00:43:10.830
So I think one really
good self-care tip is,

00:43:10.830 --> 00:43:15.170
thinking about what will
you want to be possible

00:43:15.170 --> 00:43:16.590
for yourself in the future?

00:43:16.590 --> 00:43:19.050
And what resources can you give

00:43:19.050 --> 00:43:21.000
that version of yourself today?

00:43:21.000 --> 00:43:24.260
'Cause then you benefit
from that in the present

00:43:24.260 --> 00:43:26.000
and you benefit from that in the future.

00:43:26.000 --> 00:43:27.380
- Yeah, that's so good.

00:43:27.380 --> 00:43:28.530
I really love that perspective

00:43:28.530 --> 00:43:30.710
